title: Hanoi's Train Street — is it still open? (2026)
format: story
charstyle: nutshell
voice: af_heart
music: upbeat
score: upbeat@1 cafe@2 emotional@5 upbeat@7 emotional@6 upbeat@9
sources: Wikipedia "Hanoi Train Street" (1902 French line; residents since 1956; Oct 2019 closure; reopened 2023); seasia.co 22 Jul 2026 (alley ~4 m wall to wall, ~1.5 m rail-to-wall; barricades Sept 2022); VietNamNet 11 Aug 2026 (7 Aug 2026 a train knocked a tourist's phone at Nhà Dầu, Văn Miếu ward; café owner cited for selling in the railway safety corridor; legal corridor 8.6 m each side, homes 3–4 m from the track); Hanoi Times / Hanoi Tourism Dept (Mar 2025: agencies asked to stop tours; notice 6 Feb 2025); Znews/Mekong ASEAN 6 Jan 2026 (Hanoi asks to end passenger trains Hà Nội–Gia Lâm; cafés since 2018); CAND 5 Feb 2026 (Hải Phòng trains start/end at Gia Lâm, Lào Cai trains via Văn Điển, Long Biên bridge repairs, no end date); VnEconomy 28 Feb 2026 & VnExpress 4 Mar 2026 (freight to stop, passenger trains reduced, target before Jul 2026). Checked Oct 2026.
// ANGLE   The most filmed street in Hanoi is illegal to sit on — and in 2026 its famous stretch lost its passenger trains.
// HERO    Dave (our backpacker) wants THE Train Street moment. BUT the law says no; THEREFORE crackdowns; BUT the stools come back;
//         BUT a train takes a phone; THEREFORE 2026 changes the line — Dave waits for a train that never comes; THEREFORE better ways.
// LOOK    one illustrated set (side view across the tracks), a cast that acts, real train, wind, foam, lanterns. Info lives IN the scene.
// COLOUR  sunset gold → sepia past → daylight rule → night quiet → sunset bridge → bright carriage → Ha Giang dusk.
// SOUND   crossing bell before every train, horn, crowd laugh on the foam moustache, glass crack on the phone, clock ticks in the wait.
// VIRAL   "this is Dave" cold open + a train in 6 s; a twist (no trains); a save card acted out; open loop to Ha Giang.

// 0:00 Dave sips egg coffee on a stool by the rails, eyes closed, happy. Bell rings → Co Hoa yanks his stool back → the train roars
//      past an arm's length away (tape: 1.5 m), lanterns whip, Dave keels back → foam moustache, dizzy; the far café laughs.
# vis kind=ts act=hook bellCap=1 trainCap=2 head="1.5 METRES|between a train and your coffee"
This is Dave. Dave is drinking egg coffee… on a railway.
That bell means one thing.
Move.
One and a half metres. That's Hanoi's Train Street.
// 0:13 the frame drains to sepia and rewinds: rails hammered in (1902), laundry and a running kid (1956), Co Hoa carries out the
//      first table (2018), then a wave of phones fills the far side — flashes everywhere.
# vis kind=ts act=history trans=match tc=.5,.55 tc2=.5,.6
In 1902, the French laid this line.
In 1956, railway families moved in beside it.
In 2018, someone put out a coffee table.
And then… everyone came.
// 0:27 back to daylight: an officer marches in and unrolls a tape from the rail — 8.6 m sweeps through Dave, the table, the houses;
//      the zone blinks red; whistle; Dave shrugs.
# vis kind=ts act=rule trans=whip head="8.6 METRES|the railway safety corridor"
But there's a problem.
By law, a railway keeps eight point six metres clear on each side.
These homes are three to four metres away.
So Dave's stool? Inside the corridor.
// 0:40 the crackdowns, each year lands like a stamp: tourists scatter (2019), a barrier boom bonks Dave (2022), a tour flag turns
//      around (2025)… and the stools pop right back.
# vis kind=ts act=crack trans=slices
October 2019: visitors banned.
September 2022: barricades.
March 2025: tours told to stop.
And the stools… came right back.
// 0:51 Lena leans out to film; horn; the train's edge clips her phone; it spins away; the officer writes the café a ticket.
# vis kind=ts act=phone trans=whip head="AUGUST 2026|a train took her phone"
Then, in August 2026, a tourist leaned in for the shot.
The train took her phone.
She was fine. The café owner got the ticket.
// 1:01 night on the northern stretch: Dave waits, coffee in hand; the clock spins; a sign drops (Hai Phong → Gia Lam); a cat strolls
//      across the empty rails; Dave yawns.
# vis kind=ts act=quiet trans=bloom tc=.5,.3 tcol=#ffd98a head="2026|the twist"
And 2026 brought the real twist.
Since February, Hai Phong trains start at Gia Lam, while Long Bien bridge is repaired.
So on the famous northern stretch, Dave can wait… and wait.
The trains you'll still see run on the south side, past Le Duan and Kham Thien.
// 1:17 lantern light blooms into sunset: Dave strolls Long Bien bridge with a bánh mì, trusses sliding past.
# vis kind=ts act=bridge trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.4 head="LONG BIEN|at sunset"
Want the view without the ticket? Walk Long Bien bridge at sunset.
// 1:23 whip into a carriage: Dave waves through the window; Co Hoa and the kids wave back from the alley.
# vis kind=ts act=window trans=whip head="OR RIDE IT|wave from the inside"
Or take a train south from Hanoi station, and wave from the inside.
// 1:29 the save card, acted out by Dave: off the rails, no stool by the track, behind the barrier, checking the timetable.
# vis kind=ts act=rules trans=blobs head="SAVE THIS"
If you still go: stay off the rails.
No seats by the track.
Stay behind the barrier.
And check that trains still run that day.
// 1:39 zoom through into the mountains: Dave on a motorbike climbing a Ha Giang switchback at dusk.
# vis kind=ts act=tease trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.5 head="NEXT|the Ha Giang Loop"
Next time, Dave rides the Ha Giang Loop…
and finds out what four days really cost.
